Hmm, actually both are defensible. But the reviewer's precise words: "among real runs, prefer a ciWorkflow-path match (score 2) over a bare non-dispatch run (score 1), citing the best-scoring freshest". The plainest reading: score dominates, freshness breaks ties. Terminal-vs-active is a separate axis from score. The original gh-816 design: "newest terminal non-cancelled first, else newest active" — that ordering exists so that a completed verdict outranks an in-flight run (a head with a green run + a re-run in flight shows green, not in_progress... wait does it? terminal first — yes, green shows).

Hmm, actually let me reconsider: is terminal-first right? If quality.yml re-run is in flight after a failure, showing "failure" (old terminal) vs "in_progress" (active)? The original gh-191-ish semantics and pre-PR code: terminal first. So within a tier, terminal first. Across tiers — the cleanest composition that honors both the reviewer's score-first preference AND the terminal-first convention:

For each score tier (2 then 1): if the tier has terminal non-cancelled runs → freshest of them is the cite. Else if tier has active → freshest active is the cite. First tier with any candidates wins.

That means: score-2 active beats score-1 terminal (tier 2 is checked first, has active only → cite active). That's Option A. I'll go with Option A — score tier first, then terminal-before-active within tier. It's the most defensible reading of "prefer a ciWorkflow-path match... citing the best-scoring freshest", it fixes all three enumerated mis-cite modes that involve a score-2 run existing, and it keeps honest in-flight display. And the residual risk (score-1-only heads) stays documented in the comment — which is exactly the reviewer's "at minimum".

Wait, actually there's one more wrinkle: does tier-2-active-beats-tier-1-terminal risk regressing the #1437-shaped repro test? In that test, mycheck.yml (score 2, ciWorkflow=mycheck.yml) is terminal success, review leg is a leg (not real). Cite = mycheck.yml. Fine.

And existing suite tests (14 gh-816 tests + older tests): older tests pre-gh-816 used workflow_dispatch runs with... let me check what existing tests feed. The OLD headRuns filter was `r.event === 'workflow_dispatch'` — old tests feed dispatch runs. After gh-816, dispatch runs with no path fail open as real (score 1). If a test has two dispatch runs, one matching ciWorkflow path (score 2) and one without path (score 1), the cite may change. I need to run the suite and see. Tests where the intent was "freshest dispatch wins" with mixed paths could now resolve differently — I'll check failures and adjust carefully (adjusting tests is legitimate here since semantics deliberately tightened, but per TDD rules I should write NEW failing tests first for the new behavior, then fix code, then reconcile any existing tests that encoded the old looser behavior).

3. **Thread 3 (SUGGESTION)** — carried-record fallback hardening in merged_recent: when carrying `prevCard.checks`, prefer a record with terminal conclusion; if only active, mark `carried: true`. The reviewer offers two options: drop it, or keep with explicit `carried: true` marker so the board renders it as unverified rather than live. The safer choice for audit trail (the whole point of gh-816 persistence) is keep + `carried: true`. Also: "audit fields should stay uniform across cite/auxiliary/carried records" (thread 4 note). So carried records keep their shape (they're copies of runCite) plus `carried: true`.

Implementation: 
```js
var prevChecks = (prevCard && prevCard.checks) ?
    Object.assign({}, prevCard.checks) : null;
// prefer terminal...
var mergedChecks = (pr.head && pr.head.sha && headVerdict(pr.head.sha)) || null;
if (!mergedChecks && prevChecks) {
    if (prevChecks.conclusion != null) {
        mergedChecks = prevChecks;
    } else {
        // active/in-flight record — carry but mark unverified
        mergedChecks = prevChecks; mergedChecks.carried = true;
    }
```

Hmm wait — "prefer a record with a terminal conclusion (`conclusion != null`); if only an active record exists, either drop it or keep it with an explicit `carried: true`". Since there's only ONE prev record (prevCard.checks), "prefer terminal" just means: if terminal → carry as-is; if active → carry with `carried: true` marker. I'll implement: carry terminal as-is; carry active with `carried: true`.

Hmm, but should a carried record keep `verdict: 'in_progress'`? The board's laneOf only applies to open PRs; merged cards' checks.verdict is display-only. With `carried: true` the board can render unverified. Keep verdict as-is (honest: it WAS in flight at last sight) + marker. Also — should the marker apply to terminal carried records too? The reviewer: "when carrying, prefer a record with a terminal conclusion; if only an active record exists, either drop it or keep it with an explicit carried: true marker". So terminal carried = fine without marker (it's a resolved verdict). Active carried = marked. I could also mark terminal carried records for uniform auditability... but that changes the existing test "the record rides the merged card tick-over-tick" which asserts checks.runId === 99 — marker wouldn't break that. Let me keep it minimal: marker only on active-carried (per the reviewer's words), which is the case that can mislead.

Wait, also thread 4 says: "Same applies if comment 3's carried marker lands — audit fields should stay uniform across cite/auxiliary/carried records." So the carried record should have uniform audit fields — it's a copy of runCite, so it already has runId/name/sha/conclusion. Good. And auxiliary should use `verdict` instead of `state` (thread 4 core ask).

4. **Thread 4 (SUGGESTION)** — rename `auxiliary.state` → `verdict` (matching runCite). Keep `at`/`url`/`runId`/`name`/`sha`. Should auxiliary also carry `conclusion`? runCite has conclusion; "audit fields should stay uniform across cite/auxiliary/carried records" — let me make auxiliary = a runCite-shaped record too! Actually simplest uniform approach: `out.auxiliary = runCite(leg)` — that gives verdict/at/url/runId/name/sha/conclusion/runStartedAt/updatedAt — exactly uniform vocabulary. That's elegant and satisfies "keeps one vocabulary". 

Now the TDD approach. New tests needed (RED first):

Test A (thread 1): e2e through smAgent tick — rule-level `ciWorkflow` override reaches the builder. Setup: rule with `ciWorkflow: 'rulecheck.yml'`, jobParams without ciWorkflow (or with a different default). Runs: dispatch of `.github/workflows/rulecheck.yml` on the head + maybe a leg. Assert published card cites the rulecheck run (runId match). Under current code, ciWorkflow forwards 'quality.yml' → rulecheck dispatch has a path that doesn't match → classified leg → no cite → test RED. After fix → GREEN.

