{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT (thread 2) — fixed: preference tightened to score tiers, residual risk documented**

Implemented the suggested tightening — classification untouched, preference made tier-aware in `headVerdict` (`js/factoryState.js`):

- **Score 2** — the machine's own validation workflow (`isValidationWorkflowPath(r.path, ciWorkflow)` match, i.e. `.github/workflows/<ciWorkflow>`).
- **Score 1** — every other real run (repo CI, dynamic PR-validation, and yes: any unrelated `push`/`pull_request`/`schedule` workflow the repo-wide list happens to carry).

The **best available tier cites**; within the winning tier the newest terminal non-cancelled run decides (gh-191: cancelled never cites), else the newest active run. So:

- a `labeler` run completing 20s after `quality.yml` can no longer steal the discharge cite — score beats recency (new test `the validation workflow outranks a FRESHER unrelated repo workflow`);
- an **in-flight** validation dispatch also outranks a green unrelated run, so `pr_validation` lights on the real validation in flight, never fronted by an unrelated green (new test pins the card landing in `pr_validation` with `verdict: 'in_progress'`);
- an unrelated workflow only cites when nothing better exists — that residual fail-open is now **pinned by a test** and **owned in the classifier comment**: `event !== 'workflow_dispatch'` explicitly means "any repo workflow", not "validation", with the rationale (dynamic per-PR validation workflows have arbitrary paths; a path allowlist cannot express them).

Four existing suite tests asserted the old recency-only ordering *among* real runs (e.g. freshest push-CI beating the `quality.yml` dispatch); their expectations were updated to the tightened spec, with same-tier recency/truth coverage preserved (freshest-within-tier, red-beats-older-green). `run_factoryState` 100/0, full `run_all` 2204/0.
